Here is a complete, usable C program that combines the key ideas:

- animated 0/1 motion using `frame`
- ANSI cursor control
- a glowing center region
- blue edges, purple middle, orange center
- a clean exit with `Ctrl+C`

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

#define WIDTH  90
#define HEIGHT 30

volatile sig_atomic_t running = 1;

void handle_exit(int signal_number) {
    (void)signal_number;
    running = 0;
}

int main(void) {
    int frame = 0;

    signal(SIGINT, handle_exit);

    // Clear screen, move cursor home, hide cursor.
    printf("\033[2J\033[H\033[?25l");

    while (running) {
        printf("\033[H");

        for (int y = 0; y < HEIGHT; y++) {
            for (int x = 0; x < WIDTH; x++) {
                int center_x = WIDTH / 2;
                int center_y = HEIGHT / 2;

                int dx = x - center_x;
                int dy = y - center_y;
                int distance_squared = dx * dx + dy * dy;

                /*
                 * Two moving diagonal streams.
                 * frame makes them move over time.
                 */
                int stream_a = (x + y + frame) % 11;
                int stream_b = (x - y + frame / 2) % 17;

                int bit = (stream_a == 0 || stream_b == 0) ? 1 : 0;

                /*
                 * Add a pulse in the center every few frames.
                 * This makes the center feel alive rather than static.
                 */
                int pulse = (frame / 4) % 20;
                int near_pulse = distance_squared > (pulse * pulse)
                              && distance_squared < ((pulse + 3) * (pulse + 3));

                if (near_pulse) {
                    bit = 1;
                }

                // Select color based on distance from the center.
                if (distance_squared < 100) {
                    printf("\033[38;5;208m%d\033[0m", bit); // orange center
                } else if (distance_squared < 450) {
                    printf("\033[38;5;135m%d\033[0m", bit); // purple middle
                } else {
                    printf("\033[38;5;24m%d\033[0m", bit);  // dark blue edges
                }
            }

            printf("\n");
        }

        fflush(stdout);

        frame++;
        usleep(70000); // About 14 frames per second
    }

    // Restore a normal terminal state before exiting.
    printf("\033[0m\033[?25h\033[H\n");

    return 0;
}
```

Compile it:

```bash
gcc binary_field.c -o binary_field
```

Run it:

```bash
./binary_field
```

Stop it with:

```text
Ctrl+C
```

Useful adjustments:

```c
#define WIDTH  90
#define HEIGHT 30
```

Change these to fit your terminal window. If the display wraps onto a new line, reduce `WIDTH`.

```c
usleep(70000);
```

Lower values make the animation faster:

```c
usleep(30000);
```

Higher values make it slower:

```c
usleep(150000);
```

To make the binary streams denser, reduce the modulo values:

```c
int stream_a = (x + y + frame) % 7;
int stream_b = (x - y + frame / 2) % 11;
```

To make them sparser, increase them:

```c
int stream_a = (x + y + frame) % 18;
int stream_b = (x - y + frame / 2) % 25;
```

The practical design pattern is:

```c
bit = pattern(x, y, frame);
color = color_based_on_distance_from_center(x, y);
draw(bit, color);
```

That structure makes it easy to replace the `0` and `1` output later with letters, symbols, system-status messages, or C-programming keywords while keeping the same animated visual field.

